result.summary:
I fixed the P2 where attach-one left a pane dead after a Claude Code update respawned the session under a new PID. The three P3s from the same review are fixed too. It is one commit, f6db1f1, on fix/attach-respawn in /home/agentops/dev/mdm-fix-attach-respawn. The targeted tests pass (44 run, OK), and `git merge-tree` shows no conflicts with fix/bg-settings, fix/review-wait or fix/lane-repair.

The main change is in /home/agentops/dev/mdm-fix-attach-respawn/workflow/interactive.py. attach-one no longer needs the old PID to be alive before it waits. Once a background id has been checked live and attached, and no stop is recorded, these are waited out within REATTACH_LIMIT:
- a failing listing, with any error from inventory(), including TransientInfraError;
- a row still registering its PID;
- a row reported "Session is not attachable" in a state that is not terminal (for example `starting`);
- a row still listing a dead PID, for 30 seconds (DEAD_PID_GRACE_SECONDS) from when attach-one first sees it; after that the old refusal "Native process is unavailable; refusing implicit restart" is raised.

After any attach ends, a live row with a different PID counts as a respawn and is reattached, even after exit 0, because an operator's detach never changes the PID. A detach followed by a gap waits until a row shows whether the PID changed.

These are still refused at once:
- a first attach;
- a successful listing without the id once the attached process has ended;
- a `stopped` or `failed` row;
- every identity refusal.

Nothing is attached without a live row that passed the identity checks.

To do this, `observe(sessions, node, attached)` now raises `SessionGap`, or its subclass `ProcessEnded`, for a gap and returns only a live row. New constants: DEAD_PID_GRACE_SECONDS, TERMINAL_STATES and PROCESS_ENDED. The loop tracks the attached row, the time a dead PID was first seen, and the last error.

The P3 fixes:
- A new `stop_reported()` helper runs at the top of each loop and again right after observe(), just before each `claude attach`.
- `recorded_stop()` now also returns `confirmed` and `background_id`. An unconfirmed stop prints: "The controller is stopping <node> (requested at <time>, not yet confirmed); not attaching. Inspect it with `claude logs <id>` or `claude attach <id>`."
- The give-up message ends with "; last error: …".

In /home/agentops/dev/mdm-fix-attach-respawn/workflow/test_interactive.py, AttachOneTests uses real `sleep 60` processes for the old and new PIDs, plus an injected clock and sleep. It now covers:
- respawn cases A to D reattaching the same background id; A also passes through a `starting` row;
- exit 0 followed by a changed PID reattaching while the old process lingers;
- the dead-PID grace running out into the refusal, for both a reaped PID and a zombie;
- a vanished id and a stopped or failed state refused with no sleep;
- the stop check before attach, on the first attach and on a reattach;
- the unconfirmed-stop wording;
- the give-up message carrying the last error.

Before the implementation, all of these failed for the reasons the review described.

Existing tests I adjusted:
- The dead-PID case moved out of test_a_session_gone_without_a_recorded_stop_is_refused_once into the new grace test.
- The unconfirmed-stop assertion uses the new wording.
- The give-up regex now expects the last error.
- test_a_connection_that_held_starts_a_new_count got one more clock value, because the clock is now read after every attach.

In /home/agentops/dev/mdm-fix-attach-respawn/workflow/RUNBOOK.md I extended only the attach-one paragraph. It now says an update respawns idle sessions onto the new binary about 15 s after the restart and attach-one follows them. It also covers the dead-PID grace, the terminal-state refusals, the give-up message naming the last error, the unconfirmed-stop message, and the stop check before each attach. README needed no change.
